{
  "module": "Training: how the numbers are learned",
  "lesson": {
    "slug": "gradient-descent",
    "title": "Gradients and backpropagation",
    "summary": "How training knows which way to change each parameter: the gradient of the loss, the small step against it, and backpropagation, the algorithm that computes all the gradients.",
    "covers": ["gradient-backprop"],
    "prerequisites": [
      {
        "lesson": "training-basics",
        "reason": "The gradient is a gradient of the loss, so parameters, training and loss must already be clear."
      }
    ]
  },
  "screens": [
    {
      "ref": "which-way",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A model has billions of parameters and one loss number. To lower the loss, training must decide, for "
            },
            {
              "text": "each parameter",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ", whether to make it a little bigger or a little smaller."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Guessing would take forever. Instead there is a way to measure the effect of every parameter on the loss."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 11)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "gradient",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The "
            },
            {
              "text": "gradient",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " of the loss for a parameter says how much the loss changes when that parameter is nudged. It is the derivative of the loss with respect to the parameter."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "text": "A "
              },
              {
                "text": "positive",
                "bold": true
              },
              {
                "text": " gradient: making the parameter bigger makes the loss bigger."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "A "
              },
              {
                "text": "negative",
                "bold": true
              },
              {
                "text": " gradient: making the parameter bigger makes the loss smaller."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "A gradient near "
              },
              {
                "text": "zero",
                "bold": true
              },
              {
                "text": ": nudging the parameter hardly matters."
              }
            ]
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 11)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "toy-nudge",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A toy case with one parameter "
            },
            {
              "text": "w",
              "italic": true
            },
            {
              "text": " and an invented loss, (w − 3)². At w = 1 the loss is 4, and the gradient is -4."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Nudge w up by 0.01, to 1.01. The loss becomes 3.9601, a change of -0.0399. That is about the gradient times the nudge: -4 × 0.01 = -0.04."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The gradient is negative, so making w bigger made the loss smaller."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 11)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "step",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Gradient descent",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " uses this. Move each parameter a small step "
            },
            {
              "text": "against",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " its gradient: down if the gradient is positive, up if it is negative."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "In the toy case, with a step of 0.1 times the gradient, w moves from 1 to 1 − 0.1 × (-4) = 1.4. The loss falls from 4 to 2.56."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The step is kept small on purpose. The gradient describes the loss only very near where you are standing."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 11)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "everything-at-once",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A real model does this for every parameter at the same time. One training step means billions of gradients, and then billions of small adjustments."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Repeat that step many times and the loss keeps falling, which is what training is."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 10, 11)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "backprop",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Working out billions of gradients one by one would be far too slow. "
            },
            {
              "text": "Backpropagation",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " is the algorithm that computes all of them efficiently."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "It applies the chain rule of calculus "
            },
            {
              "text": "backward",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " through the network, from the loss back towards the first layer. That is the "
            },
            {
              "text": "backward pass",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " from the last lesson."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Backpropagation only computes the gradients. Gradient descent is what then uses them to change the parameters."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 10, 11)"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
